            <ArticleTitle>High density lipoprotein cholesterol and apolipoprotein A-I are persistently elevated during long-term treatment with pitavastatin, a new HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>320-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ijcard.2008.11.130</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Although a low level of high density lipoprotein-cholesterol (HDL-C) is an important risk factor for coronary heart disease, few available agents are capable of significantly increasing HDL-C. This multicenter study demonstrated that administration of pitavastatin, a new HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor, significantly and persistently increased HDL-C (from 36.0+/-5.9 to 40.5+/-9.1 mg/dL: p&lt;0.001) and apolipoprotein A-I levels (from 108.4+/-18.0 to 118.7+/-19.3 mg/dL: p&lt;0.01) in 43 hypercholesterolemic patients with low HDL-C over the course of 12 months of treatment. This suggests that pitavastatin may contribute to reduction in coronary heart disease.</AbstractText>
